<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</h1><p class="st-meta">781 CE – 888 CE · capital Pavia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The former Lombard kingdom under Charlemagne's dynasty, which made Italy part of the Frankish world and linked its crown to the imperial title.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard Kingdom</a> in 774 and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a>. In 781 Pope Hadrian I crowned his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin</a> King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, creating a subordinate kingdom with its capital at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a>, covering northern and central Italy apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the papal lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> law and the palace at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a> but installed Frankish counts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and supported monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobbio-abbey">Bobbio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nonantola-abbey">Nonantola</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin</a>'s son Bernard rebelled in 817 and was blinded in 818, dying of his injuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II</a> ruled Italy as emperors; Louis II spent decades fighting Arab raiders in the south and recovered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> in 871.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II</a> died in 875, the crown passed among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloman-of-bavaria">Carloman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, who was deposed in 887 and died in 888. Italian nobles then chose their own kings, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-i-of-italy">Berengar of Friuli</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> continued under them.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy.jpg" alt="Carolingian Kingdom of Italy" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo, eagle by N3MO · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Holy_Roman_Emperor_with_haloes_(1430-1806).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>781 CE – 888 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pavia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pepin of Italy, Lothair I, Louis II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Italy (Carolingian)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=834&amp;focus=carolingian-kingdom-of-italy" aria-label="Carolingian Kingdom of Italy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4898 583 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carolingian Kingdom of Italy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5218 839L5139 672M5218 839L5218 839M5218 839L5218 839"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139